-- Q914: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Gifting the interest to his daughter transfers the suspended losses to her for immediate deduction against her own income","Selling 60% of the interest releases a proportional $108,000 of suspended losses while preserving the remaining 40% for future passive income","Selling the entire interest to an unrelated buyer releases all $180,000 of suspended losses because a fully taxable disposition triggers full recognition","Retaining the interest allows Mercer to offset suspended losses against future passive income without triggering the $50,000 built-in loss on sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 469(g)(1), a fully taxable disposition of an entire passive activity interest to an unrelated party releases all suspended passive losses for deduction against nonpassive income. Selling 100% to an unrelated buyer releases the full $180,000, plus the $50,000 capital loss ($200,000 FMV minus $250,000 basis) is also deductible.
Wrong (A): Under Section 469(j)(6), a gift does not release suspended losses for deduction — they are added to the donee''s basis instead.
Wrong (B): A partial sale of 60% releases only the proportionate share ($108,000), leaving $72,000 still suspended.
Wrong (D): Retaining the interest defers any deduction indefinitely and depends on uncertain future passive income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 914;

-- Q922: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The suspended losses transfer to the daughter for her to deduct against her own income","The donor deducts $30,000 of suspended losses on her current-year return","The basis of the gifted property is increased by $30,000, bringing it to $80,000","The suspended losses are permanently lost and cannot benefit either party"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 469(j)(6), when a passive activity interest is transferred by gift, suspended losses are not allowed as a deduction. Instead, the losses increase the donor''s basis immediately before the gift: $50,000 + $30,000 = $80,000, which becomes the donee''s carryover basis under Sec. 1015.
Wrong (A): Suspended losses do not transfer to the donee for deduction — they are absorbed into basis.
Wrong (B): A gift does not trigger recognition of suspended losses, unlike a sale under Sec. 469(g).
Wrong (D): The losses are not permanently lost — they provide indirect benefit through the increased basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 922;

