-- Q1228: Capital Structure Tax Planning [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The strategy is advisable because staying below the $29 million threshold exempts the full $1,500,000 interest deduction, saving $315,000 in tax","The strategy is unnecessary because the small business exception applies per entity, and Thornfield can split into two entities to stay below the threshold","The strategy is not advisable because the $63,000 tax benefit from avoiding disallowed interest is dwarfed by deferring $840,000 of revenue-related tax","Revenue deferral has no effect on the gross receipts test because the threshold is based on cash receipts, not accrual-method revenue"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Without the small business exception, Section 163(j) limits interest deductions to 30% of ATI. The disallowed amount is only $300,000 ($1,500,000 minus 30% of $4,000,000), costing $63,000 in current tax — and it carries forward indefinitely. Deferring $4,000,000 of revenue delays $840,000 of tax and risks IRS challenge under the economic substance doctrine or Section 446 method-change rules.
Wrong (A): The disallowed interest is $300,000 (not $1,500,000), and the carryforward means the cost is timing, not permanent.
Wrong (B): Aggregation rules under Section 448(c)(2) combine related entities, so splitting does not avoid the threshold.
Wrong (D): The gross receipts test under Section 448(c) applies to gross receipts as determined under the taxpayer''s accounting method, including accrual-method revenue.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1228;

-- Q1234: Capital Structure Tax Planning [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because RedCorp independently has average annual gross receipts below the $30 million threshold","No, because controlled group members are aggregated and the combined $50 million exceeds the $30 million threshold","Yes, because each member of a controlled group tests the gross receipts threshold independently","No, because controlled group members are permanently prohibited from deducting any business interest expense"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 448(c)(2), all members of a controlled group or affiliated group are treated as a single employer for the gross receipts test. BlueCorp''s $40 million plus RedCorp''s $10 million equals $50 million, exceeding the $30 million threshold, so neither entity qualifies for the Section 163(j) small business exception.
Wrong (A): RedCorp cannot be tested independently — the aggregation rules require combining all controlled group members'' gross receipts.
Wrong (C): The statute explicitly requires aggregation; separate testing is not permitted for controlled group members.
Wrong (D): Controlled group members can deduct interest expense — it is simply limited to 30% of ATI under Section 163(j) when the small business exception does not apply.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1234;

-- Q1237: Nontaxable Dispositions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both properties share a two-year replacement period and must meet the ''similar or related in service or use'' functional-use standard","The warehouse must be replaced within two years under the functional-use test, while the condemned lot gets three years and a broader like-kind standard under Section 1033(g)","Both properties qualify for a three-year replacement period because the federally declared disaster extends the deadline, and both use the like-kind standard","The warehouse gets a four-year disaster replacement period while the condemned lot has two years, and both require the functional-use standard"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1033, casualties and theft require replacement with property "similar or related in service or use" (the narrower functional-use test) within two years. Condemnation of real property held for business or investment triggers Section 1033(g), which applies the broader like-kind standard and a three-year replacement period. A warehouse replaced with a retail building would likely fail the functional-use test.
Wrong (A): Condemnation of real property uses the broader like-kind standard under Section 1033(g), not the functional-use test, and gets three years.
Wrong (C): The disaster provision may extend deadlines but does not change the replacement standard from functional-use to like-kind for casualties.
Wrong (D): The replacement periods and standards are reversed — the warehouse gets two years (not four), and the condemned lot uses like-kind (not functional-use).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1237;

-- Q1245: Nontaxable Dispositions [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Use the installment method to defer all $200,000 of gain recognition over the payment period","Request a private letter ruling from the IRS before proceeding with the installment sale","Donate the asset to a qualified charity instead of completing the installment sale","Elect out of the installment method to recognize all gain currently and offset it with the $150,000 capital loss carryforward"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 453(d), a taxpayer may elect out of the installment method on the return for the year of sale, recognizing the entire $200,000 gain currently. With $150,000 of capital loss carryforwards under Section 1212(b), the net taxable gain is only $50,000. This strategic timing maximizes use of carryforwards that might otherwise expire.
Wrong (A): Deferring gain via installments risks the carryforwards expiring unused under Section 1212(b)''s five-year limitation.
Wrong (B): A private letter ruling is unnecessary — the election out of installment reporting is a statutory right under Section 453(d).
Wrong (C): Donating the asset does not allow the taxpayer to utilize the capital loss carryforwards, which can only offset capital gains.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1245;

-- Q3921: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The deferral election must be made before the beginning of the taxable year in which compensation is earned","The plan must be funded through a secular trust holding assets for participants","The employee must agree to forfeit all deferred amounts upon voluntary termination","The employer must deposit deferred amounts into a segregated escrow account annually"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 409A, deferral elections for NQDC plans must be made before the beginning of the taxable year in which the services giving rise to the compensation are performed. Failure to comply triggers immediate income inclusion plus a 20% additional tax and interest.
Wrong (B): NQDC plans are typically unfunded promises to pay — funding through a secular trust would cause immediate taxation under the economic benefit doctrine.
Wrong (C): Section 409A does not require forfeiture upon termination; it governs election timing and distribution events.
Wrong (D): Depositing amounts into escrow would constitute funding, which is inconsistent with NQDC plan tax deferral requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3921;

-- Q3932: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["NSOs create an AMT adjustment at exercise while ISOs do not trigger any alternative minimum tax consequences","ISOs have no regular income tax recognition at exercise if statutory holding periods are met, whereas NSOs are taxed as ordinary income at exercise","ISOs provide the employer with a compensation deduction at exercise while NSOs generate no employer deduction","NSOs require a two-year holding period from the grant date while ISOs have no mandatory holding period requirement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 421(a), no regular income tax is recognized upon exercise of an ISO if statutory holding periods are met. By contrast, under Sec. 83(a), NSO exercise triggers ordinary income equal to the spread between FMV and exercise price. The ISO spread is an AMT preference item under Sec. 56(b)(3).
Wrong (A): It is ISOs, not NSOs, that create an AMT adjustment at exercise under Section 56(b)(3).
Wrong (C): The employer receives a compensation deduction for NSOs under Sec. 162, not ISOs — ISOs generate no employer deduction unless a disqualifying disposition occurs.
Wrong (D): ISOs require a two-year holding period from grant and one year from exercise; NSOs have no statutory holding period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3932;

-- Q3937: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Property or services provided by an employer that would be deductible as a business expense if the employee paid for them directly","An employer-provided vehicle used exclusively for personal commuting and non-business purposes","Free meals provided for the convenience of the employer on the employer''s business premises","Health insurance premiums paid by the employer as part of the employee''s benefits package"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 132(d), a working condition fringe benefit is any property or service provided by the employer to the extent the employee could have deducted the cost as a trade or business expense under Section 162 or depreciation under Section 167. Examples include business-use vehicles, professional dues, and subscriptions.
Wrong (B): A vehicle used exclusively for personal purposes does not qualify because personal commuting is not deductible under Section 162.
Wrong (C): Employer-provided meals on business premises are excluded under Section 119 as a de minimis or convenience-of-employer fringe, not the working condition fringe rules.
Wrong (D): Employer-paid health insurance is excluded under Section 106, a separate exclusion provision from the working condition fringe benefit rules.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3937;

