UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Full funding, vesting, and fiduciary requirements under ERISA Title I apply to top-hat plans without exemption","Only the reporting and disclosure requirements of ERISA apply, with exemptions from participation, vesting, and funding rules","No ERISA requirements of any kind apply to top-hat plans because they cover only select management employees","Top-hat plans must satisfy the same participation, vesting, and funding requirements as tax-qualified retirement plans"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ERISA Sec. 201(2) and 301(a)(3), top-hat plans — unfunded NQDC plans for a select group of management or highly compensated employees — are exempt from ERISA''s participation, vesting, funding, and fiduciary requirements. However, they remain subject to reporting and disclosure provisions and must file a one-time statement with the Department of Labor.
Wrong (A): Top-hat plans are specifically exempt from funding, vesting, and fiduciary rules under ERISA''s statutory carve-outs.
Wrong (C): Top-hat plans are not entirely exempt — they must comply with ERISA''s reporting and disclosure requirements under Part 1 of Title I.
Wrong (D): Top-hat plans are intentionally exempted from qualified plan requirements to allow flexible executive compensation arrangements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3953;

-- Q3958: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Salary earned from a full-time job as a software engineer","Dividends received from a publicly traded stock portfolio","Net rental income from a residential property in which the taxpayer does not materially participate","Interest income earned from a savings account at a bank"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 469, passive income includes income from trade or business activities in which the taxpayer does not materially participate and rental activities, which are generally per se passive with limited exceptions. Net rental income from a property without material participation is classified as passive income.
Wrong (A): Salary is active (nonpassive) income because the taxpayer materially participates in employment activities.
Wrong (B): Dividends from publicly traded stocks are portfolio income under Section 469, not passive income.
Wrong (D): Interest income from a savings account is portfolio income, which is distinct from passive income under the Section 469 framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3958;

-- Q3993: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because the taxpayer spent more than 500 hours on the rental activity during the year","No, because the taxpayer''s AGI exceeds $75,000 and the special allowance is fully phased out","Yes, because the taxpayer materially participated in the rental activity through 600 hours of service","No, the rental remains passive because the taxpayer is not a real estate professional, but the $15,000 loss may be deducted under the $25,000 active participation allowance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): The taxpayer does not qualify as a real estate professional under IRC Section 469(c)(7) because more than half of personal services (2,000 hours engineering vs. 600 hours rental) were not in real property trades or businesses. Rental activities remain per se passive. However, with active participation and AGI of $85,000 (below the $100,000 phase-out threshold), the full $15,000 loss is deductible under the $25,000 special allowance.
Wrong (A): Spending 500+ hours does not convert a rental activity to nonpassive without meeting the real estate professional requirements.
Wrong (B): The $25,000 allowance begins phasing out at $100,000 AGI, not $75,000 — at $85,000, the full allowance is available.
Wrong (C): Material participation in a rental activity does not override the per se passive classification without real estate professional status.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3993;

-- Q4002: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The grantor avoids all income tax on the annuity payments received from the GRAT during the term","The annuity payments made by the GRAT are deductible by the grantor on the income tax return","The GRAT assets receive a stepped-up basis at the grantor''s death during the trust term","The remainder has a gift tax value of approximately zero while future appreciation passes transfer-tax-free to beneficiaries"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): A zeroed-out GRAT is structured so the present value of the annuity payments approximately equals the value of the transferred assets, making the taxable gift of the remainder interest approximately zero under IRC Section 2702. If the trust assets appreciate faster than the Section 7520 rate, the excess growth passes to remainder beneficiaries free of gift and estate tax.
Wrong (A): As a grantor trust, the grantor pays income tax on trust earnings — the annuity payments themselves are not separately taxable, but the grantor is not avoiding all income tax.
Wrong (B): GRAT annuity payments are not deductible — they are returns of the grantor''s own property from a grantor trust.
Wrong (C): If the grantor dies during the GRAT term, the trust assets are included in the estate under Section 2036, not given a stepped-up basis as a planning benefit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4002;

-- Q4008: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both spouses must consent to split all gifts made by either spouse during the entire calendar year","Both spouses must be United States citizens at the time the gift splitting election is made","The gifts must be made exclusively to the spouses'' children or other lineal descendants","Gift splitting is available only for gifts that individually exceed the $100,000 threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 2513, gift splitting requires that both spouses consent to split all gifts made by either spouse during the calendar year. Both must be married at the time of the gift and must file Form 709 to make the election. With splitting, each spouse is treated as making half the $80,000 gift ($40,000 each).
Wrong (B): Both spouses need not be U.S. citizens — the election is available as long as both are married to each other at the time of the gift.
Wrong (C): Gift splitting applies to gifts made to any donee, not just children or lineal descendants.
Wrong (D): There is no minimum gift amount for gift splitting — the election applies regardless of the gift''s size.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4008;

-- Q4009: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The remainder passing to the children has a reduced gift tax value because the charitable lead interest reduces the present value of the taxable gift","The donor receives a current income tax deduction equal to the full $500,000 contributed to the CLAT","The annual charity payments of $40,000 are deductible by the children as beneficiaries of the trust","The trust assets are excluded from the donor''s gross estate regardless of when the donor dies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): A CLAT provides a gift or estate tax benefit by reducing the taxable value of the remainder interest passing to non-charitable beneficiaries. The taxable gift equals the FMV of trust assets minus the present value of the charitable lead interest, calculated using the Section 7520 rate. If trust assets grow faster than the 7520 rate, excess appreciation passes to the children transfer-tax-free.
Wrong (B): A non-grantor CLAT does not provide a current income tax deduction to the donor; a grantor CLAT provides a one-time deduction but the donor is taxed on trust income annually.
Wrong (C): The charity payments are made by the trust, not deductible by the remainder beneficiaries.
Wrong (D): Estate inclusion depends on the type of CLAT and whether the donor retains any interest — it is not automatically excluded.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4009;
